--TEST--
Core\Password::verify refuses a stored hash whose passes times memory is past its work ceiling, before one pass runs, and never quotes the row — the module doc's *`verify` bounds what the stored hash may ask for*
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `t` arrives inside the stored string, exactly as `m` does, so it is a lever
// on this process's time. The ceiling is memory times passes: 216 passes over
// this class's own 19 MiB is the first count past it, and a million passes is
// the row an attacker would write.
secret string $password = "correct horse battery staple";
string $firstPast = '$argon2id$v=19$m=19456,t=216,p=1$jJ+hokoSJRsAzYsgfwhV6g$BGyWXoH11l0/GF4ezLqvtQgPY0T/4fv4DukErq9R0cI';
string $endless = '$argon2id$v=19$m=19456,t=1000000,p=1$jJ+hokoSJRsAzYsgfwhV6g$BGyWXoH11l0/GF4ezLqvtQgPY0T/4fv4DukErq9R0cI';

array<string> $rows = [$firstPast, $endless];
foreach ($rows as string $stored) {
    try {
        bool $matched = Core\Password::verify($password, $stored);
        echo "ran every pass the row asked for\n";
    } catch (RuntimeError $tooCostly) {
        echo $tooCostly->message, "\n";
    }
}

// The ordinary row is unaffected: a hash this class writes is far inside it.
string $fresh = Core\Password::hash($password);
if (Core\Password::verify($password, $fresh)) {
    echo "a hash made now still verifies\n";
}
--EXPECT--
Core\Password::verify(): $hash asks for 216 passes over 19456 KiB, past this class's ceiling of 4194304 KiB of passes — no hash it writes needs that
Core\Password::verify(): $hash asks for 1000000 passes over 19456 KiB, past this class's ceiling of 4194304 KiB of passes — no hash it writes needs that
a hash made now still verifies
